8
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:34,680
In the chemistry laboratory, a common task we will face is that of separating the components

9
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:40,120
of a mixture. Just like in nature, reactions in the lab produce mixtures of solids, liquids,

10
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:45,880
and gases. In order to isolate a compound from a product mixture, some kind of separation

11
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:50,440
technique is required, and the appropriate technique will depend on the phases and

12
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:54,840
characteristics of the components we are trying to separate. Today we'll be looking

13
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:59,720
at filtration. This is a fast and easy way to separate solids from liquids.

14
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:04,600
The way a filter works in the lab is just like a coffee maker in your kitchen.

15
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:10,200
You pour solid items into a filter, and the liquids go through while the solids stay on top.

16
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,960
Filters come in many different varieties, but today we'll be looking at the two main

17
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:20,200
types of filtration used in general and organic chemistry labs. These are gravity filtration,

18
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,840
and vacuum filtration, and they are named after the specific mechanism used

19
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:29,560
to move the liquid through the filter. Let's examine these techniques one at a time.

20
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:34,360
With gravity filtration, we're using the gravitational force, as the name implies.

21
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:39,000
We pour the mixture into the filter and let gravity do the work. This technique is

22
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,960
commonly used when we want to remove impurities from a product, or when we want to remove a

23
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:49,080
drying agent that has been added to a compound in the liquid phase, meaning a solid that

24
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:55,240
coordinates to water molecules and removes them from solution. In either case, this means

25
00:01:55,240 --> 00:02:00,280
that it is specifically the liquid that is the target we want to isolate. For a gravity

26
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:06,360
filtration setup, we'll need a ring stand, some clamps, a glass funnel, filter papers,

27
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:12,200
and a clean flask or beaker to collect the filtered liquid. This liquid is called the filtrate.

28
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:17,000
First you'll need to fold the filter paper so that it can fit in the funnel,

29
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,600
as the filter paper is what will separate the components of the mixture,

30
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:26,440
since it has tiny pores that let liquids through, but not solids. There are a few methods we can

31
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:31,320
use to fold the paper. What we certainly don't want is to leave an open hole in the filter,

32
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:36,600
like this, because the mixture will go through unfiltered. The most common method for folding

33
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:41,240
the filter paper is to start with the circle like this, then fold it in half,

34
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:51,660
and then fold it in half again like this. Now comes the fun part. You open the outer

35
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:59,380
layer and make a cone that will easily fit into the funnel. A more advanced way to fold the paper

36
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:04,660
is called the fluted filter paper method. You can do this by folding the paper this way into

37
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:11,860
eight sections, and then putting it into the funnel like this. This maximizes the surface

38
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:16,740
tension of the filter and makes the liquid go through much faster. For most students in a

39
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:22,180
general chemistry laboratory course, the first method will be just fine. After folding the

40
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:27,540
filter paper, you're now ready to assemble the filtration setup. Add the filter paper

41
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:34,140
to the funnel. If you find that it does not stay in place, wetting it slightly with the

42
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:39,340
same solvent that is present in the mixture that is to be filtered will cause it to adhere to

43
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:45,820
the glass. This is usually water or some organic solvent. You'll need to wet the paper evenly using

44
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:51,580
a squirt bottle, and be careful not to leave any dry spots or bubbles. With the setup done and

45
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:57,340
our mixture handy, we are now ready to start the filtration. We'll begin by making sure our solution

46
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:02,860
is well mixed and ready to go. After giving it a swirl to generate a suspension, you'll gently

47
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:07,420
pour it into the filter setup. You should start to see the liquid move through the filter and

48
00:04:07,420 --> 00:04:13,580
slowly drop into the receiving flask. If at any point you see some powder or solid in the flask,

49
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:18,460
your filter is perforated or incorrectly folded, and you will have to start again with a new

50
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:24,060
receiving flask. When introducing the mixture to the funnel, always pour slowly and wait for

51
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:29,660
the level of the liquid to go down before you pour some more. Do not pour so much that the

52
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:35,100
level approaches the top of the filter. If the filter overflows, some of the mixture will

53
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:41,100
reach the receiving flask unfiltered. Continue slowly and keep pouring the mixture until you're

54
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:47,820
done. Sometimes you'll have some powder left at the bottom or on the sides of the original flask.

55
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:52,700
You can wash it with the appropriate solvent and pour that into the flask like this.

56
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:56,140
This is an important step, as you don't want to lose any of your product.

57
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:03,420
Once the dropping stops, the filtration is complete and you have successfully separated

58
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:09,900
your liquid from the solid. Gravity filtration is a great way to separate small mixtures that

59
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:14,060
go through the filter quickly, but if you have a large volume of the mixture it can

60
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:19,180
be very time consuming. This is one of the situations where vacuum filtration becomes

61
00:05:19,180 --> 00:05:24,460
desirable. Vacuum filtration is just like gravity filtration, except that in addition

62
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:30,460
to using gravity to drive the separation, we utilize a vacuum pump as well. A vacuum pump

63
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:36,300
is used to drive the air out of the flask and create a pressure differential between the inside

64
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:42,060
and outside of the flask. This causes atmospheric pressure to push the liquid through the filter

65
00:05:42,060 --> 00:05:46,700
much more quickly. For vacuum filtration, the setup will be slightly different. We will need

66
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:53,100
a special flask called a Büchner flask. This has a line out that will be attached to the vacuum.

67
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:58,860
We'll also need a rubber hose and a vacuum line. Many chemistry laboratories have vacuum lines

68
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:04,780
built into the workstations, but a standalone vacuum pump can also be used. And we certainly

69
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:09,740
will also need a Büchner funnel. This is attached to a rubber stopper so that it can

70
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:15,020
make a tight seal to maintain vacuum. Again, we will need a ring stand and clamp.

71
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:21,740
And finally, we'll need some filter paper, which is of a size that fits into our Büchner funnel,

72
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:25,980
making sure to cover all of the holes, but not quite touching the perimeter,

73
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:30,860
so that it can lay perfectly flat on the bottom of the funnel. If your paper is too big,

74
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:36,460
you can cut it using scissors until it is an appropriate size. Again, make sure you don't

75
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:41,900
leave any of these holes exposed, or your unfiltered mixture will go through and contaminate the

76
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:47,820
filtrate. To assemble the setup, connect your Büchner flask to the ring stand and clamp.

77
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:51,500
Be sure to clamp it tightly or it may fall over during the filtration.

78
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:58,460
Then attach your vacuum line to the flask. Add your funnel to the flask and make sure that

79
00:06:58,460 --> 00:07:04,780
it creates a tight seal, otherwise the vacuum won't work. And finally, add your filter paper,

80
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:12,380
and then wet it with the appropriate solvent. First, turn on the vacuum slowly and make sure

81
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:17,020
it's working. You'll know that it's working when you see the paper stick tightly to the funnel.

82
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:21,260
If it's not working, that means there is a leak in your system, so make sure you check your

83
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:26,940
flask setup and your vacuum line connection. If the solvent is highly volatile, it may dry

84
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:33,100
out quickly once the vacuum is on, so just apply more solvent. Next, you pour your mixture into

85
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:38,140
the funnel and watch it go through. It should be much quicker than gravity filtration. If it's

86
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:43,820
too slow, turn up the vacuum to make the filtration go faster. Make sure you don't turn it up too high,

87
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:48,380
as this can rip your filter paper and all the solid will go through. If that happens,

88
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:53,020
or if you see any solid in your filtrate for any other reason, you can just get a new

89
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:58,540
clean Buechner flask, new filter paper, and repeat the filtration. Now, just like with

90
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:03,100
gravity filtration, you keep pouring your solution into the funnel until you're done.

91
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:08,620
Make sure the funnel doesn't overflow, and make sure your filter paper doesn't get dislodged from

92
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:13,900
the pores, as the mixture will go through if that happens. Additionally, you must be aware of

93
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:19,420
the level of the filtrate accumulating in the Buechner flask. We do not want this to approach

94
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:25,420
the side arm, or it will get sucked into the vacuum. If it gets too high, turn off the vacuum,

95
00:08:25,420 --> 00:08:30,700
transfer the filtrate into some other flask, reconnect the filtration setup, and resume.

96
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:37,180
Your solid should now be depositing nicely on top of your filter paper, like this. After you're

97
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:42,780
done, let the vacuum line run longer to dry the product some more. In the end, you'll be left

98
00:08:42,860 --> 00:08:48,620
with any solids on top of your filter paper, and any liquid in your Buechner flask. In some

99
00:08:48,620 --> 00:08:54,060
experiments, our desired product will be the solid, and in others it will be the liquid filtrate.

100
00:08:54,060 --> 00:09:00,220
Sometimes you may need both. But when the product is a solid, vacuum filtration is often specifically

101
00:09:00,220 --> 00:09:06,300
desirable over gravity filtration, as the vacuum will help the solid dry very quickly,

102
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:11,340
and it can be very easily scraped off with a spatula for further drying and analysis.

103
00:09:14,330 --> 00:09:19,930
So that's how you perform gravity filtration and vacuum filtration. We'll be using these

104
00:09:19,930 --> 00:09:25,130
techniques many more times in this series, especially during experiments where we synthesize

105
00:09:25,130 --> 00:09:30,250
something. With any synthesis, we will always have to separate the target compound from a

106
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:35,770
mixture of unreacted starting material, side products, and solvent, in order to obtain

107
00:09:35,770 --> 00:09:41,130
a pure product. But for now, let's move forward and examine some more separation techniques.

108
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:50,820
Thank you very much to my channel for more tutorials. Support me on Patreon so I can keep making content, and as always, feel free to email me
